==English==
{{wp|+ (finance)}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|hair cut}}

===Etymology===
From {{compound|en|hair|cut}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈhɛə(ɹ)kʌt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-haircut.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The act of [[cut]]ting of the hair, often done professionally by a [[barber]], [[hair stylist]], or [[beautician]].
# The [[style]] into which the hair is cut.
#: {{syn|en|hairdo|hairstyle}}
# {{senseid|en|partial loss financially}} {{lb|en|legal|business}} A partial [[loss]], [[financially]]: thus, in a [[bankruptcy]] proceeding, the proportional reduction in the [[debt]] that will be paid to each [[creditor]] (based on an evaluation of the total debt owed and the total [[asset]]s of the [[debtor]]); among investors, the negative [[return on investment]] from a losing proposition.
#: {{co|en|to take a '''haircut'''}}
#* {{RQ:en:New York Times|author=Thomas L. Friedman|title=Haircut Time|date=1998-01-17|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1998/01/17/opinion/foreign-affairs-haircut-time.html|passage=And he needs to make sure that any American bank that was involved in loans to Asia, and now wants to share in the I.M.F. bailout, takes a '''haircut'''. And I'm not just talking about a trim.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20220726150136/https://www.nytimes.com/1998/01/17/opinion/foreign-affairs-haircut-time.html|archivedate=26 July 2022}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|url=http://www.theguardian.com/business/2005/mar/04/argentina.internationalnews|title=Argentina says debt default is over|date=2005-03-04|author=Ashley Seager|passage=Its president, Nestor Kirchner, was expected to confirm overnight that about 75% of the country's bondholders had agreed to swap their old debt for new at a loss of up to 70 cents in the dollar—also a record and one that Mr Kirchner has called “the biggest '''haircut''' in history” for investors.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261026225812/https://www.theguardian.com/business/2005/mar/04/argentina.internationalnews|archivedate=26 October 2026}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2016|author=w:Yanis Varoufakis|title=And the Weak Suffer What They Must?{{...|: Europe's Crisis and America's Economic Future}}|publisher=Hachette UK|isbn=9781568585055|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=gg45DgAAQBAJ&pg=PT187&dq=%22haircut%22+Greece&hl=&cd=9&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22haircut%22%20Greece&f=false|text=Something similar was happening at the same time in Greece. In the spring of 2012 Greece's public debt did, eventually, take a '''haircut''', confirming that an unpayable debt will receive a '''haircut''' whatever the dogmas of European officials.}}
#* {{RQ:FT|edition=weekend|author=Tabby Kinder|date=2024-03-16|title=Reddit IPO reveals reality check for Silicon Valley|section=Companies & Markets|page=14|passage=And there is a growing consensus that it is finally time for founders to swallow their pride, accept a  huge '''haircut''' to valuation and help establish a new floor for their stock to start growing again, helping to encourage all the companies coming up behind them to do the same.}}
# {{lb|en|finance}} The difference between the value of a [[loan]] and the value of its [[collateral]].
#: {{syn|en|margin}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|editors=Samuel N. Cohen; et al.|title=Stochastic Processes, Finance and Control{{...|: A Festschrift in Honor of Robert J. Elliott}}|publisher=World Scientific|isbn=9789814383318|page=190|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=62a6CgAAQBAJ&pg=PA190&dq=%22haircut%22+treasury+bills&hl=&cd=8&source=gbs_api#v=onepage&q=%22haircut%22%20treasury%20bills&f=false|text=Government securities having high credit rating such as Treasury bonds and Treasury bills are usually subjected to 1% to 10% '''haircut''', while for more risky, volatile or illiquid securities, such as a stock option, the '''haircut''' might be as high as 30%.}}

